{:edb/db-before-t 2692 :edb/db-after-t 2693 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536005 50 #inst "2026-10-15T17:48:44.462Z" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186124439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186556105 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186124439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186556111 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186124439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186556113 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186124439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186556118 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186124439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186556132 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186124439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186556140 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186124439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186556144 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186124439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186556160 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186124439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186556162 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186124439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186556166 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186124439 1040 #edb/ref 17592186556172 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556102 1080 "⟨0,-6,1⟩" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556102 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556102 1083 #uuid "015bc2bf-13ce-5c4f-8f0c-a4a74231f0c3" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556103 1080 "⟨0,1,-6⟩" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556103 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556103 1083 #uuid "044116f0-96fa-511a-a093-d3f451857cf0" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556104 1080 "⟨4,0,-1⟩" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556104 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556104 1083 #uuid "04fcaa4c-cdc7-55cc-9fbc-bf020baece7a" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556105 1068 #edb/ref 17592186556134 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556105 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556105 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,2,0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=(-2)x$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556105 1072 #uuid "055263bb-00e6-5ecd-9052-3f0991382dda" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556105 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-2,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-2,2) & =(-2)(-2)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,2) & ={f}_{x}(-2,2)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(0) & =(4)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(0) & =4 x + 8 \\\\ z & =4 x + 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{y=2 \\\\ z=4 x + 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=4 x + 8$ (or $4 x - z + 8=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨4,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨4,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =- i - 4 k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-4⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(⟨-1,0,-4⟩)=⟨1,0,4⟩$ is also parallel to it." 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556106 1080 "⟨0,16,1⟩" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556106 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556106 1083 #uuid "0a2d6f59-2293-503c-954b-099ede3ec4ab" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556107 1080 "⟨3,0,1⟩" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556107 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556107 1083 #uuid "0c3e1e95-6abb-5b2d-b333-2c9928f10fe8" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556108 1080 "⟨0,1,-2⟩" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556108 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556108 1083 #uuid "10ba9c0a-1858-5d2b-ae82-2126392ec58e" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556109 1080 "⟨1,0,2⟩" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556109 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556109 1083 #uuid "13415e5b-d7ce-5839-ac8a-9fdda0db113d" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556110 1080 "⟨0,6,-1⟩" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556110 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556110 1083 #uuid "1882e685-d6cd-57e2-8865-032f24c20593" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556111 1068 #edb/ref 17592186556137 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556111 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556111 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=4.$ Given that the point $P(-2,4,-4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=3$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556111 1072 #uuid "24dbd77f-2590-58ef-821c-6bd1e8a62bdc" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556111 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=4.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-2,4).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-2,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-2,4) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,4) & ={f}_{x}(-2,4)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(-4) & =(3)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(-4) & =3 x + 6 \\\\ z & =3 x + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,4,-4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{y=4 \\\\ z=3 x + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=4,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=3 x + 2$ (or $3 x - z + 2=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨3,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =- i - 3 k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-3⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(⟨-1,0,-3⟩)=⟨1,0,3⟩$ is also parallel to it." 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556112 1080 "⟨1,0,0⟩" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556112 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556112 1083 #uuid "252f91f1-8db7-563d-92ee-dfc8b2e40f76" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556113 1068 #edb/ref 17592186556129 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556113 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556113 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=3.$ Given that the point $P(3,3,-3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=(-2)y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556113 1072 #uuid "322047e2-3976-5636-bcd1-e92ee6b570c4" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556113 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(3,3).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(3,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(3,3) & =(-2)(3)=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(3,3) & ={f}_{y}(3,3)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =(-6)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =18 - 6 y \\\\ z & =15 - 6 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(3,3,-3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{x=3 \\\\ z=15 - 6 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=15 - 6 y$ (or $- 6 y - z + 15=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,-6,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,-6,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j - 6 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,-6⟩.$" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556114 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556106 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556114 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556122 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556114 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556145 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556114 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556154 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556114 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556158 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556114 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556114 1077 "selection" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556114 1078 #edb/ref 17592186556122 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556114 1079 #uuid "35a54cb9-6e14-5105-89d2-938f4cb3495a" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556115 1080 "⟨0,1,-8⟩" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556115 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556115 1083 #uuid "37dd67a6-9302-52ce-a4eb-b458c3c38495" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556116 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556143 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556116 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556147 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556116 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556149 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556116 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556156 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556116 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556163 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556116 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556116 1077 "selection" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556116 1078 #edb/ref 17592186556147 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556116 1079 #uuid "39fe770b-a34b-5bb0-b24e-2cb8ef45a74d" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556117 1080 "⟨4,0,1⟩" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556117 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556117 1083 #uuid "3aaf0daf-4db1-56a4-85c0-b9d13bb393d5" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556118 1068 #edb/ref 17592186556133 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556118 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556118 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=3.$ Given that the point $P(3,3,-3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=-2$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556118 1072 #uuid "48d0933d-38f4-5fca-ac17-2cb0bd7b696b" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556118 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(3,3).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(3,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(3,3) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(3,3) & ={f}_{y}(3,3)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =(-2)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =6 - 2 y \\\\ z & =3 - 2 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(3,3,-3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{x=3 \\\\ z=3 - 2 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=3 - 2 y$ (or $- 2 y - z + 3=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,-2,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,-2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j - 2 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,-2⟩.$" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556119 1080 "⟨0,2,-1⟩" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556119 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556119 1083 #uuid "49af77ba-23b1-5c02-ae78-e0a7f3dcabf1" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556120 1080 "⟨1,0,-2⟩" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556120 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556120 1083 #uuid "4abd2572-3ff6-52e7-b470-d37775dc5e9a" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556121 1080 "⟨0,1,2⟩" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556121 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556121 1083 #uuid "4e3b10f7-1ab2-5dd0-bdc2-2c023a53bdc4" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556122 1080 "⟨0,1,16⟩" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556122 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 131941