$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j + 2 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,2⟩.$" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556141 1080 "⟨-2,0,1⟩" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556141 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556141 1083 #uuid "88e51073-51d9-5a66-b57f-a46e981e04ac" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556142 1080 "⟨0,1,0⟩" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556142 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556142 1083 #uuid "89d7a47f-5ffb-5fb7-8773-673d0bdca988" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556143 1080 "⟨-6,0,-1⟩" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556143 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556143 1083 #uuid "8c44812f-aa8f-5a3b-a2c9-3a9af8c5a229" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556144 1068 #edb/ref 17592186556114 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556144 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556144 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,2,-3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=(8)y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556144 1072 #uuid "8f308815-8a1f-5bcf-b629-6357db90aa05" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556144 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,2) & =(8)(2)=16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,2) & ={f}_{y}(1,2)(y-(2)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =(16)(y-(2)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =16 y - 32 \\\\ z & =16 y - 35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,2,-3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{x=1 \\\\ z=16 y - 35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=16 y - 35$ (or $16 y - z - 35=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,16,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,16,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 16 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j + 16 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,16⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,16⟩.$" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556145 1080 "⟨0,1,-16⟩" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556145 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556145 1083 #uuid "91cf613e-e775-536e-b2b2-d6c5690147d2" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556146 1080 "⟨0,1,-6⟩" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556146 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556146 1083 #uuid "9afc3218-bbc9-5554-9630-980dd247d59b" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556147 1080 "⟨1,0,-6⟩" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556147 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556147 1083 #uuid "9d66afe9-4f16-5fcb-ad94-4581dad49b13" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556148 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556115 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556148 1075 #edb/ref 