39536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556160 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=4.$ Given that the point $P(-2,4,-4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=(3)x$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556160 1072 #uuid "dd9d8206-b563-5e86-ab9a-f167bb3c1e2b" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556160 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=4.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-2,4).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-2,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-2,4) & =(3)(-2)=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,4) & ={f}_{x}(-2,4)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(-4) & =(-6)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(-4) & =- 6 x - 12 \\\\ z & =- 6 x - 16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,4,-4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{y=4 \\\\ z=- 6 x - 16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=4,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=- 6 x - 16$ (or $- 6 x - z - 16=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨-6,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-6,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =- i + 6 k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,6⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(⟨-1,0,6⟩)=⟨1,0,-6⟩$ is also parallel to it." 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556161 1080 "⟨0,1,2⟩" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556161 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 131