$⟨-1,0,2⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(⟨-1,0,2⟩)=⟨1,0,-2⟩$ is also parallel to it." 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556163 1080 "⟨-6,0,1⟩" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556163 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556163 1083 #uuid "e85558f7-5bb9-5e70-8631-2dd61ed3184f" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556164 1080 "⟨0,1,-2⟩" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556164 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556164 1083 #uuid "ee0f23c0-bab4-5a6d-b985-d5db0527d0d3" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556165 1080 "⟨1,0,0⟩" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556165 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556165 1083 #uuid "f2e01698-f591-5f18-9466-8532bda73ad2" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556166 1068 #edb/ref 17592186556135 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556166 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556166 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-3.$ Given that the point $P(-3,3,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=(2)y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556166 1072 #uuid "f350a280-28d9-5c60-b2e4-617d820b96d3" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556166 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-3,3).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-3,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-3,3) & =(2)(3)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-3,3) & ={f}_{y}(-3,3)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(6)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =6 y - 18 \\\\ z & =6 y - 19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-3,3,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{x=-3 \\\\ z=6 y - 19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=6 y - 19$ (or $6 y - z - 19=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,6,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,6,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j + 6 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,6⟩.$" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556167 1080 "⟨1,0,-4⟩" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556167 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556167 1083 #uuid "f703c35e-0bb7-5ae6-ac28-2ef517196952" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556168 1080 "⟨0,6,1⟩" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556168 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556168 1083 #uuid "f9241366-8977-556e-a838-84ae8f1a7a55" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556169 1080 "⟨1,0,4⟩" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556169 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556169 1083 #uuid "fee29037-72b8-57de-8e34-703bacf1d7c4" 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556170 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556109 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556170 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556120 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556170 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556123 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556170 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556141 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [17592186556170 1075 #edb/ref 17592186556159 13194139536005 17592186045443 true] [1759218655617